course: dictionary
lesson: 13642
title: Words that show what must happen
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Inevitably is an adverb. It tells you that something was certain to happen, and nobody could stop it. You could say it means "as you would expect".
explain: «The sky went dark and the wind began to blow. Inevitably, it started to rain.» The dark sky and the wind are the clues. When the sky looks like that, rain is what you would expect, so it could not be avoided.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Inevitably, it started to rain» becomes «It started to rain, as you would expect.» It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: Inevitably means in a way that cannot be avoided or stopped.
fact: «Inevitably, the ice cream melted in the hot sun.» The melting was certain to happen.
fact: «Skipping practice inevitably leads to mistakes.» Inevitably leads to means that one thing always results in another.
fact: Writers use inevitably to show that a result was certain, so the reader is not surprised by it.
exercise: «Inevitably, the ice cream melted in the hot sun.» What does inevitably mean here? | It happened very quickly | *It was certain to happen, as you would expect | It happened only sometimes | It happened by accident, and nobody planned it
exercise: «Skipping practice inevitably leads to mistakes.» What does the phrase inevitably leads to tell you? | One thing might happen one day, but nobody knows | Nobody can tell what will happen next | *One thing always results in another | A mistake is a good thing
exercise: Which phrase means the same as inevitably? | Far away from home | Once in a while | Only if you are lucky | *As you would expect
test: «Inevitably, the bus was late because of the snow.» What does inevitably mean here? | *Bound to happen, and could not be avoided | Happened on a sunny day | Happened only once | Happened by chance
test: Which sentence shows a result that could not be avoided? | I chose the red pen by accident. | Maybe the dog will come back later. | She sometimes forgets her lunch. | *Inevitably, the tide came in and covered the sand.
exam: «After three days of rain, the river inevitably flooded the fields.» What does inevitably mean? | It flooded only for a moment | *As you would expect, it could not be avoided | It flooded by mistake | It never flooded at all
